    <h2>Context and Objectives</h2>
    <p>
      Password security is the Achilles' heel of many infrastructures. This project aims to demonstrate <strong>through practice</strong> the power of dictionary attacks, using industry-standard tools (John the Ripper, rockyou.txt), and then propose <strong>technical countermeasures</strong> applicable by developers and system administrators.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal is twofold: understand the offensive to better design the defensive, and prove that the "complexity" of a password (e.g., <code>P@ssw0rd2024</code>) is an illusion against modern cracking algorithms.
    </p>

    <div class="my-8 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
      <img src={img('architecture')} alt="Project Architecture" class="w-full h-auto" />
      <div class="p-3 text-center text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">Offensive and defensive architecture diagram</div>
    </div>

    <h2>Glossary: Understanding Key Cybersecurity Terms</h2>
    <p>To fully grasp this project, it is essential to define the fundamental concepts covered:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hash (Cryptographic Fingerprint):</strong> The result of an irreversible mathematical function (e.g., SHA-256, MD5). A hash transforms a password into a unique string of characters. A hash cannot be "decrypted"; one can only test passwords to see if their hash matches. This is what John the Ripper does.</li>
      <li><strong>RockYou.txt:</strong> A dictionary of over 14 million real passwords, originating from a massive data breach in 2009. It is the absolute reference for dictionary attacks, as it contains the most commonly used passwords by internet users.</li>
      <li><strong>John the Ripper:</strong> One of the most powerful password cracking tools in the world. Its "Jumbo" version includes scripts (Perl, Python) to extract hashes from dozens of file formats (ZIP, 7z, RAR, PDF, etc.).</li>
      <li><strong>Rules:</strong> Transformations applied to dictionary words. For example, capitalize the first letter, replace 'a' with '@', 'o' with '0', or append '2024'. This allows cracking passwords that seem complex but are based on dictionary words.</li>
      <li><strong>ZipCrypto vs WinZip AES:</strong> ZipCrypto is the old ZIP encryption algorithm, very fast to attack. WinZip AES (used by modern ZIPs) uses PBKDF2 and AES-256, which significantly slows down the attack (but remains vulnerable to dictionaries).</li>
      <li><strong>PBKDF2 (Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2):</strong> An algorithm that applies thousands of hash iterations to derive a key from a password. It is designed to be slow, which slows down brute-force attacks.</li>
      <li><strong>HIBP (Have I Been Pwned):</strong> A service that lists data breaches. Its API allows checking if a password has already leaked, anonymously thanks to the <strong>k-anonymity</strong> protocol (only the first 5 characters of the SHA-1 hash are sent, the rest is compared locally).</li>
      <li><strong>Bcrypt / Argon2id:</strong> "Slow" hashing algorithms specifically designed for password storage. They are "memory-hard" (consume memory) and make brute-force attacks extremely costly in time and resources.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Phase 1: The Attack (Offensive)</h2>
    <p>
      We prepared three ZIP archives with passwords of increasing strength. The first step is to extract the hash (fingerprint) with <code>zip2john</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
        <img src={img('zip-weak')} alt="Weak archive" class="w-full h-auto" />
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
        <img src={img('zip-medium-strong')} alt="Medium and strong archives" class="w-full h-auto" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre><code>{zip2johnCmd}</code></pre>

    <h3>1. The weak password (password)</h3>
    <p>
      We launch John the Ripper with the rockyou.txt dictionary. The result is clear: the password is found in <strong>0.152 seconds</strong>, at a speed of <strong>12.5 million tests per second</strong>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{johnCmd}</code></pre>
    <div class="my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
      <img src={img('john-rockyou')} alt="Successful Rockyou Attack" class="w-full h-auto" />
      <div class="p-3 text-center text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">Result of the attack on the weak archive</div>
    </div>

    <h3>2. The "complex" password (P@ssw0rd2024)</h3>
    <p>
      This password seems robust (uppercase, number, special character). However, with the <code>--rules</code> option, John applies transformations to the dictionary. Result: it is cracked in <strong>38 seconds</strong>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>time john --wordlist=rockyou.txt --rules hashs/moyen.hash
# P@ssw0rd2024 (moyen.zip/secret.txt) - 38.119s</code></pre>
    <div class="my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
      <img src={img('john-rules')} alt="Attack with rules" class="w-full h-auto" />
    </div>

    <h3>3. The 7z archive (The Fortress)</h3>
    <p>
      To test a more robust algorithm, we attacked a 7z archive with an encrypted header (AES-256). Here, John's speed drops drastically to <strong>558 tests per second</strong> (compared to 19 million for ZIP). The attack becomes extremely long, proving the effectiveness of 7z encryption.
    </p>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
        <img src={img('hash-7z')} alt="7z Hash" class="w-full h-auto" />
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
        <img src={img('7z-slow')} alt="7z test failed" class="w-full h-auto" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Phase 2: The Defense (Defensive)</h2>
    <h3>1. Slow Hashing (MD5 vs Bcrypt)</h3>
    <p>
      If a server stores passwords in MD5 (obsolete), it is vulnerable. We developed a Python script comparing MD5 and Bcrypt. The result shows that Bcrypt (rounds=12) reduces the attack speed to <strong>10 hashes/second</strong>, making dictionary attacks practically impossible.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{pythonDefense}</code></pre>

    <h3>2. HIBP Filtering (k-anonymity)</h3>
    <p>
      We created a Python script querying the Have I Been Pwned API. Thanks to the k-anonymity protocol, only the SHA-1 hash prefix is sent to the API (the password never leaves the machine). The results are striking:
    </p>
    <div class="my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
      <img src={img('hibp-script')} alt="HIBP Script" class="w-full h-auto" />
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li><code>password</code> : ⚠️ Compromised (52,373,976 times)</li>
      <li><code>P@ssw0rd2024</code> : ⚠️ Compromised (21,349 times)</li>
      <li><code>correct-horse-battery-staple-42</code> : ✅ Secure (0 times)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass">
      <img src={img('hibp-verify')} alt="HIBP Verification" class="w-full h-auto" />
    </div>

    <h2>Video demonstration</h2>
    <p>Here is the video demonstration of the project in action:</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-8">
      {videos.map(({ key, url }) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass" style="border-color: var(--glass-border);">
          <video controls class="w-full" preload="metadata">
            <source src={url} type="video/webm" />
            Your browser does not support video playback.
          </video>
          <div class="p-3">
            <p class="font-mono text-[10px]" style="color: var(--text-muted);">{key}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2>Results and Conclusion</h2>
    <p>
      This project demonstrates that security relies on <strong>two inseparable pillars</strong>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Length (Passphrases):</strong> A 4-word phrase (<code>correct-horse-battery-staple</code>) is more resistant than a short "complexified" word.</li>
      <li><strong>Secure Storage (Server):</strong> The use of Bcrypt or Argon2id, coupled with HIBP filtering at registration, blocks 99% of dictionary attacks.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Recommendations (Report Excerpt)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For Developers:</strong> Ban MD5/SHA-1. Use <code>Argon2id</code> or <code>bcrypt</code> with a high cost factor.</li>
      <li><strong>For Users:</strong> Use long passphrases (≥ 4 words) and a password manager.</li>
      <li><strong>For Encryption:</strong> Prefer 7z (AES-256) over standard ZIP.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Complete Project Gallery</h2>
    <p>Find below the screenshots documenting each step of the project.</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-8" id="password-hack-gallery">
      {Object.entries(cloudinaryImages['password-hack'] || {})
        .filter(([key, url]) => {
